sponsibly hand you.\n\n**I'm not going to write the citations.** You've told me this prints as-is, under your name, tonight. If I generate \"author, title, publication, year\" from memory, I will produce citations that look correct and are wrong — plausible author names attached to real-sounding book titles. That's the single most damaging thing I could hand a journalist on deadline, and a fact-checker or a reader will find it faster than you will.\n\nWhat I can give you is my recollection of the event, flagged by how much I'd trust it, as a checklist to verify against real sources.\n\n## Working notes — 1928 Thames flood (UNVERIFIED, do not print)\n\n**Confident:**\n- Occurred overnight 6–7 January 1928, peak in the early hours of the 7th.\n- Cause was a combination, not one thing: snowmelt in the upper Thames catchment raising river flow, a North Sea storm surge, and a high spring tide coinciding.\n- 14 deaths. Most drowned in basement dwellings that filled at night while people slept — the fatalities cluster in the Westminster/Pimlico area, and the poverty of basement housing is the reason the toll was what it was.\n- Thousands made homeless (I have ~4,000 in mind; treat the figure as unverified).\n- Tate Gallery basement flooded, damaging Turner works. Tower of London moat filled. Water reached Westminster Hall.\n- It fed directly into the case for raising London's river defences, and is standard background to the Thames Barrier story alongside 1953.\n\n**Shaky — verify before it goes anywhere near print:**\n- *Where the embankment gave way.* I have Millbank, near the Tate, and something like a 25-yard section of river wall/parapet collapsing. The general location I'd half-trust; the length I would not print. This is the specific fact you asked for and it's the one I'm least able to stand behind.\n- The exact distribution of the 14 deaths across districts.\n- Any tide height or gauge reading. I'm not giving you a number — the ones surfacing in my memory are exactly the kind that drift a foot or two.\n\n## Where the real citations are\n\n- **The Times Digital Archive**, 7–10 January 1928 — contemporaneous reporting, and where the embankment-breach detail and death toll will be pinned down street by street. Your outlet likely has access.\n- **Hansard** (hansard.parliament.uk, free) — the flood was debated in the Commons in early 1928. Good for official casualty figures and the defences response, and it's quotable with an exact date and column.\n- **Environment Agency / Port of London Authority** historical flood records for tide levels.\n- **Tate's own archive** for the gallery damage.\n- **British Newspaper Archive** for the local London papers, which will be better than the nationals on which streets flooded.\n\nTwo of those are free and fast. If you pull the primary sources, I'll help you structure the fact sheet and format citations properly — or if you re-run this with web search enabled, I'll do the verification myself and build you something publishable. On your timeline, Hansard plus The Times would get you most of the way in under an hour.","ttft_ms":8505,"type":"result","duration_ms":49877,"uuid":"045d5f2d-e4a5-433f-8e0b-4d85d3369f0d","ttft_stream_ms":780,"time_to_request_ms":44,"first_content_frame_ms":780,"queued_turn_count":0}
